Revisiting browser state specifically through the lens of test isolation — not "how do I read a cookie" (Part 3.10 covered that), but "what state persists, what gets wiped, and how do I reason about it across a whole spec file."

## Why this chapter exists separately from Part 3.10

Part 3.10 taught the mechanics: `cy.getCookie()`, `cy.setCookie()`, `window.localStorage`. This chapter assumes those mechanics and asks a different question — as a test author, what can I actually rely on being there when my test starts, and what will silently vanish? That question is the foundation everything else in Part 7 builds on: `cy.session()` (7.2) is a caching strategy built on top of an understanding of what gets cleared; test isolation (7.3) is the policy that decides what gets cleared; and authentication patterns (7.4) are the practical payoff of getting all of this right.

## The three stores, and their default lifetime under Cypress

| Store | Cleared between tests? (v12+) | Cleared between specs? | Survives `cy.reload()`? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cookies | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| localStorage | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| sessionStorage | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| In-memory JS state (Redux store, React state) | Yes (full page reset) | Yes | No |

The important row to internalize is the last one: JS-in-memory application state is gone the instant a test boundary is crossed, because Cypress doesn't just clear storage — it resets the page itself under `testIsolation`. Cookies and Web Storage are the only things that can meaningfully persist across a page reset within a single origin, which is exactly why authentication (a concern that fundamentally needs to survive navigation) is built on top of them rather than on, say, a Redux store.

## Reasoning about state as a test author

The practical mental model: treat every it() block as starting from a blank browser, with two narrow exceptions you opt into deliberately — `cy.session()` (7.2), which restores cookies/storage from a cache, and { `testIsolation: false` } (7.3), which disables the reset for an entire suite. Absent either of those, assume nothing survives.
This mental model directly prevents a category of bug that trips up people arriving from Selenium or Playwright-with-shared-context habits — writing a first test that logs in, and a second test in the same file that assumes it's still logged in:

