## Cross-origin storage — a real, sharp edge

Cookies and storage are origin-scoped by browser design, not by any Cypress decision. If your app's flow genuinely spans two origins — your app plus a separate auth domain — storage set on one origin is invisible from the other, full stop, exactly as it would be for a real user's browser. This connects directly back to `cy.origin()` (Part 9.1): storage manipulation inside a `cy.origin()` block only affects that block's origin, and Cypress's `cy.clearCookies()`/clearAllCookies()/getAllLocalStorage() (v12+) commands exist specifically to reach across origins when you need whole-browser visibility rather than single-origin scoping:

```js
cy.clearAllCookies();          // all origins the browser currently holds cookies for
cy.getAllLocalStorage().then((result) => {
  // result is keyed by origin
  expect(result['https://app.bizlevate.com']).to.have.property('authToken');
});
```

## Storage as a debugging tool, not just a state concern

Worth naming explicitly: when a test fails in a way that smells like an auth or state problem, inspecting storage directly is often faster than guessing from symptoms:

```js
cy.window().then((win) => {
  cy.log('Current localStorage:', JSON.stringify(win.localStorage));
});
cy.getCookies().then((cookies) => {
  cy.log('Current cookies:', JSON.stringify(cookies.map(c => c.name)));
});
```

Dropping these into a failing test temporarily is a legitimate, quick diagnostic step before reaching for heavier tools (Part 12.1).

## Comparison

Playwright's `storageState` (previewed in Part 3.10) makes this whole chapter's concern — "what state persists and how do I control it deliberately" — an explicit, serializable, file-based artifact from the start, rather than something you reason about implicitly through hook placement. Selenium has no isolation policy of its own at all; whatever the WebDriver session's cookie jar happens to contain persists until you explicitly clear it or the session ends, which means Selenium suites are more prone to accidental cross-test state leakage by default, not less — Cypress's aggressive default clearing is stricter than Selenium's default behavior, even though it occasionally surprises newcomers expecting session persistence.

The mechanism that reconciles two things that otherwise pull against each other: test isolation demanding a fresh login every test, and performance demanding you don't actually pay for that every time.

## The problem it solves, restated precisely

Part 6.1 established that an API login costs roughly 100–300ms versus a UI login's 3–5 seconds. But even 200ms, repeated as a real network round-trip in every single beforeEach across a 150-test suite, adds up to real time — and more importantly, it's genuinely wasteful: the login itself isn't what most of those tests are about, and re-authenticating identically every single time provides zero additional test coverage over authenticating once and restoring that state.
`cy.session()` caches the result of an authentication flow — the cookies and storage state it produces — keyed by a name you choose, and restores it on subsequent calls without re-running the login logic at all, while still fully honoring test isolation's guarantee that each test starts clean.

## Basic usage

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(role, () => {
    cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
      const { email, password } = users[role];
      cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', { email, password }).then((response) => {
        window.localStorage.setItem('authToken', response.body.token);
      });
    });
  });
});

beforeEach(() => {
  cy.loginAs('manager');
  cy.visit('/leave/approvals');
});
```

The first time `cy.session('manager', setupFn)` runs in a spec, Cypress executes setupFn in full — the real login happens. Cypress then snapshots the resulting cookies and storage. Every subsequent call to `cy.session('manager', ...)` — in later tests, even in different spec files within the same run — skips setupFn entirely and instead restores the snapshotted cookies/storage directly. No network call, no re-running your login logic — just applying the saved state.

## Why the window.localStorage assignment above actually works now

This directly resolves the subtlety flagged in Part 6.1: setting localStorage inside a `cy.session()` setup callback is captured as part of the session snapshot, unlike setting it as a bare step in a test body (which would be wiped by the next test-isolation reset). `cy.session()` is specifically designed to be the sanctioned place to establish state that should survive and be restored — it's the deliberate exception to "assume nothing persists" from 7.1.

## The validation option — making the cache trustworthy

A cached session is only useful if it's actually still valid. `cy.session()` accepts a validate function, run every time a cached session is about to be restored, to confirm the restored state is genuinely still good:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(
    role,
    () => {
      cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
        const { email, password } = users[role];
        cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', { email, password }).then((response) => {
          window.localStorage.setItem('authToken', response.body.token);
        });
      });
    },
    {
      validate() {
        cy.request({
          url: '/api/auth/me',
          headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${window.localStorage.getItem('authToken')}` },
          failOnStatusCode: false,
        }).its('status').should('eq', 200);
      },
    }
  );
});
```

If validate() fails — say, the cached token expired mid-run, or a previous test explicitly logged out and mutated the stored token — Cypress discards the cached session and re-runs the setup function fresh, transparently. This is what makes `cy.session()` safe to rely on across a long-running suite rather than a source of "works on the first test, mysteriously fails on the fortieth" bugs: staleness is actively detected and self-healed rather than silently trusted forever.

## cacheAcrossSpecs — extending the cache beyond one file

By default, a session cache lives for the duration of a single cypress run invocation but is scoped per spec file execution in some configurations; explicitly opting into cross-spec caching squeezes out further savings on large suites:

```js
cy.session(role, setupFn, {
  validate() { /* ... */ },
  cacheAcrossSpecs: true,
});
```

With this set, the very first spec file in the run pays the real login cost once, and every subsequent spec file in the same cypress run invocation restores from that same cache — genuinely amortizing the cost across the entire suite rather than per file.

## Sessions are keyed — different arguments, different caches

The session name (first argument) is the cache key. Different roles naturally get independent caches:

```js
cy.loginAs('admin');      // cached under 'admin'
```
`cy.loginAs('manager')`;    // cached under 'manager' — a completely separate session

```js
cy.loginAs('employee');   // cached under 'employee'
```

This means a spec file that needs to test behavior across multiple roles can freely switch between them, and each restore is still fast:

```js
it('a manager can see what an employee cannot', () => {
  cy.loginAs('employee');
  cy.visit('/leave/approvals');
  cy.get('[data-cy=approve-btn]').should('not.exist');   // employees can't approve
```

`cy.loginAs('manager')`;    // switches to a DIFFERENT cached session — fast, no re-login cost

```js
  cy.visit('/leave/approvals');
  cy.get('[data-cy=approve-btn]').should('exist');
});
```

You can also key sessions on more than a role name when the underlying state genuinely differs per test — an object serializes into a stable key:

```js
cy.session({ role: 'manager', team: 'engineering' }, setupFn);
```

## cy.session() resets the page — the behavior worth knowing precisely

After restoring or establishing a session, Cypress clears the page (navigates to about:blank) — it does not automatically leave you on whatever page the setup function last visited. This means `cy.session()` should be paired with an explicit `cy.visit()` afterward, which is exactly the pattern shown throughout this chapter (`cy.loginAs(role)`; `cy.visit(path)`;). Forgetting the follow-up `cy.visit()` is a common first-time mistake — the session is established correctly, but the test is left looking at a blank page.

## UI-based sessions — when login genuinely can't be bypassed via API

Not every application exposes a clean login API endpoint a test can call directly — some are behind SSO flows, some have login logic with side effects that must run through the actual UI (MFA challenges, terms-acceptance modals tied to session creation). `cy.session()` supports a full UI login inside its setup function just as well as an API call, and still gets the caching benefit — the expensive part only happens once, regardless of how expensive it is:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(role, () => {
    cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
      const { email, password } = users[role];
      cy.visit('/login');
      cy.get('[data-cy=email]').type(email);
      cy.get('[data-cy=password]').type(password, { log: false });
      cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
      cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');   // anchor: confirm login actually completed
    });
  });
});
```

This is a genuinely important pattern to know: `cy.session()`'s value isn't "always use `cy.request()` instead of the UI" — it's "pay the real cost exactly once, however expensive that real cost is." For an app where the only reliable way to authenticate is the full UI flow, this still delivers nearly all the same performance benefit as the API-shortcut version, because the 3–5 second UI login now happens once per role per run instead of once per test.

## Comparison

```js
// Playwright — storageState as an explicit, file-based, cross-run artifact
// global-setup.ts
const browser = await chromium.launch();
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto('/login');
await page.fill('[data-cy=email]', 'manager@bizlevate.test');
await page.fill('[data-cy=password]', 'pw');
await page.click('[data-cy=submit]');
await page.context().storageState({ path: 'auth/manager.json' });

// playwright.config.ts
projects: [
  { name: 'as-manager', use: { storageState: 'auth/manager.json' } },
]
```

Playwright's version is philosophically the same idea — pay the login cost once, restore many times — but implemented as an explicit file artifact generated in a separate global-setup script and wired in via project configuration, rather than an in-memory cache keyed by a string inside the test file itself. Playwright's approach persists across separate npx playwright test invocations (the file's still on disk tomorrow), whereas Cypress's `cy.session()` cache — even with `cacheAcrossSpecs`: true — lives only for the duration of one cypress run. That's a real trade-off: Cypress's version requires zero separate setup infrastructure and works immediately inside any test file; Playwright's version requires more upfront wiring but persists further and is more explicitly inspectable (you can literally open the JSON file).
Selenium has no equivalent mechanism at all. Session reuse in a Selenium suite is either hand-rolled (serialize cookies to a file yourself, load them back with driver.add_cookie() in later tests) or simply not done, with every test paying the full UI login cost — which is a large part of why Selenium-based E2E suites have a longstanding reputation for being slow at scale, beyond just the WebDriver protocol overhead itself.

The policy layer underneath everything in 7.1 and 7.2 — what `testIsolation` actually does, why it exists, and the narrow, deliberate cases where turning it off is the right call rather than a shortcut.

## What testIsolation: true actually does, precisely

This has been referenced throughout the manual (Parts 1.5, 2.1, 3.10) but deserves its full, precise treatment here. With `testIsolation: true` (the default since Cypress v12), before every single it() block, Cypress:

- Clears all cookies for the current origin (and, practically, others it's tracking)
- Clears localStorage and sessionStorage
- Navigates the page to about:blank
- Resets any in-memory application state as a consequence of that navigation (the app's JS simply isn't running anymore against a blank page)
This is not the same as a full fresh browser process per test — Cypress doesn't relaunch the browser, and the Cypress-runner-level state (aliases from a previous test, custom command registrations, config) isn't what's being reset here; specifically the page's own state is what gets wiped.

## Why this default exists, and why it's the right default

The alternative — tests silently depending on whatever state a previous test happened to leave behind — produces a specific, insidious failure mode: a test that only passes when run after another specific test, and fails (or worse, passes for the wrong reason) when run alone, in a different order, or via .only. This kind of order-dependency is one of the most expensive categories of bug in any test suite to diagnose, because the failure's symptom appears in a test that, read in isolation, looks completely correct — the actual defect is an invisible dependency on execution order that lives nowhere in the failing test's own code.
Enforced isolation makes this class of bug structurally impossible rather than merely discouraged. A test cannot accidentally depend on another test's leftover cookie, because there is no leftover cookie to depend on. This is a real, meaningful design decision that trades a small performance cost (recall Part 7.2's whole reason for existing) for a strong correctness guarantee, and it's worth stating plainly as a genuine strength of Cypress's architecture rather than merely an inconvenience `cy.session()` works around.

## The pre-v12 behavior, for context on tutorials you'll encounter

Versions before Cypress 12 cleared cookies between tests but did not clear localStorage/sessionStorage by default. This produced exactly the kind of confusing cross-test pollution described above — a token left in localStorage by one test silently authenticating a later test that never called any login logic at all, making that later test's actual assertions meaningless (it "worked," but not for the reason the test claimed). Any tutorial written before the v12 change that manually calls `cy.clearLocalStorage()` in a beforeEach is working around a gap that no longer exists — redundant, but harmless, under current defaults.

## When to disable it: { testIsolation: false }

There is a narrow, legitimate use case: a genuinely sequential, order-dependent scenario where the tests are, by their nature, steps of one continuous flow rather than independent assertions — most commonly, a multi-page wizard where re-establishing full state at every step would be both expensive and conceptually wrong, because the thing actually being tested is the continuity across steps.

```js
describe('Employee onboarding wizard (sequential)', { testIsolation: false }, () => {
  before(() => {
    cy.loginAs('admin');
    cy.visit('/employees/onboard');
  });

  it('completes step 1 — personal details', () => {
    cy.get('[data-cy=first-name]').type('Aarav');
    cy.get('[data-cy=last-name]').type('Sharma');
    cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=step-indicator]').should('contain', 'Step 2');
  });

  it('completes step 2 — employment details', () => {
    // relies on step 1's DOM state and in-memory wizard state still being present
    cy.get('[data-cy=department]').select('Engineering');
    cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=step-indicator]').should('contain', 'Step 3');
  });

  it('completes step 3 — review and submit', () => {
    cy.get('[data-cy=review-name]').should('contain', 'Aarav Sharma');   // proves step 1's data survived
    cy.get('[data-cy=finish]').click();
    cy.wait('@createEmployee');
  });
});
```

## The trade-offs you're accepting, stated honestly

- You lose independent runnability. it.only() on "completes step 3" alone will fail, because it depends on DOM/state that only exists after steps 1 and 2 ran first. This is a real cost — one of Cypress's best debugging affordances (isolate and rerun exactly the failing test) stops working for this describe block.
- You lose the "each test proves its own precondition" guarantee. If step 2 has a latent bug that silently corrupts state without failing its own assertions, step 3 might fail — or worse, pass incorrectly — for a reason that has nothing to do with step 3's actual logic. Debugging failures in a `testIsolation: false` block requires reasoning about the whole chain, not just the failing test.
- Retries become more dangerous. If retries.runMode is set (Part 1.5, Part 10.3) and step 2 fails and retries, it may retry starting from whatever state step 1's retry left behind rather than a clean slate — order-dependent retry behavior is a genuinely harder thing to reason about correctly.

## The better alternative, most of the time: one test, not several

For a genuinely continuous, single-user-journey scenario, the more idiomatic Cypress approach — and the one that avoids every trade-off above — is simply not splitting it into multiple it() blocks at all:

```js
// ✅ Preferred: one test, fully self-contained, independently runnable
it('completes the full onboarding wizard', () => {
  cy.loginAs('admin');
  cy.visit('/employees/onboard');

  cy.get('[data-cy=first-name]').type('Aarav');
  cy.get('[data-cy=last-name]').type('Sharma');
  cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();

  cy.get('[data-cy=department]').select('Engineering');
  cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();

  cy.get('[data-cy=review-name]').should('contain', 'Aarav Sharma');
  cy.get('[data-cy=finish]').click();
  cy.wait('@createEmployee');
});
```

This single test is fully independently runnable via it.only(), isolation stays enabled for the rest of the suite (this test doesn't need the describe-level override at all), and it reads as what it actually is — one continuous scenario, not three artificially separated ones pretending to be independent. { `testIsolation: false` } should be reached for rarely, and its existence in a codebase is worth treating as a flag for "was this actually necessary, or was it a workaround for a test that should have been written as one it() block."

## A genuinely legitimate remaining case: expensive shared read-only setup

```js
describe('Payroll report — read-only views', { testIsolation: false }, () => {
  before(() => {
    cy.task('seedLargePayrollDataset');   // expensive, run once
    cy.loginAs('admin');
    cy.visit('/payroll/reports/annual');
  });

  it('shows the correct total', () => { cy.get('[data-cy=total]').should('contain', 'NPR'); });
  it('shows the correct chart', () => { cy.get('[data-cy=chart]').should('be.visible'); });
  it('shows the correct breakdown table', () => { cy.get('[data-cy=breakdown-row]').should('have.length.greaterThan', 0); });
});
```

Here, none of the three tests mutate shared state, so the usual "hidden dependency between tests" risk doesn't really apply — they're independent assertions about one expensive-to-produce, unchanging page, which is a meaningfully different situation from the sequential-wizard case and a more defensible use of the override.

## Comparison

Playwright's default is philosophically identical for its own unit of isolation — each test() gets a fresh BrowserContext (effectively a fresh, isolated browser profile: its own cookies, storage, cache) unless you deliberately configure shared state via `storageState` or test.describe.serial(). Playwright's test.describe.serial() is the direct, named equivalent of Cypress's { `testIsolation: false` } — an explicit opt-in for exactly the same "these tests are really one continuous flow" scenario, with the same trade-offs around independent runnability. The two tools converged on nearly identical policy and nearly identical escape hatches, which is a reasonable signal that this represents genuine consensus on testing best practice rather than an arbitrary Cypress-specific choice.
Selenium has no built-in isolation policy of any kind — a WebDriver session persists whatever state accumulates until you explicitly tear it down (driver.delete_all_cookies(), a fresh driver.get(), or ending the session entirely), which means the order-dependency failure mode described at the top of this chapter is something Selenium suites are structurally exposed to by default, and avoiding it is entirely a matter of test-author discipline rather than anything the tool enforces.

Bringing 7.1's storage mechanics, 7.2's caching, and 7.3's isolation policy together into the concrete decision every Cypress project has to make on day one: how do logged-in tests actually get logged in.

## The two fundamental approaches, restated as a real decision

UI login — drive the actual login form: type credentials, click submit, wait for redirect. Slow, but exercises real code, including the login form itself.
Programmatic login — bypass the form entirely: call a login API directly (`cy.request()`, Part 6.1) or seed storage directly (Part 7.1), and land on an authenticated page without ever touching a login <input>.
These aren't competing options for the same purpose — they answer different questions, and a mature suite uses both, deliberately, for different tests.

## When UI login is the right choice

Testing the login form itself is, definitionally, a UI-login test — validation messages, "remember me" behavior, password visibility toggles, lockout after failed attempts, MFA challenges:

```js
describe('Login form', () => {
  it('shows an error for invalid credentials', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.get('[data-cy=email]').type('wrong@bizlevate.test');
    cy.get('[data-cy=password]').type('wrongpassword');
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=login-error]').should('contain', 'Invalid email or password');
    cy.url().should('include', '/login');
  });

  it('redirects to the dashboard on success', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.get('[data-cy=email]').type('manager@bizlevate.test');
    cy.get('[data-cy=password]').type('correctpassword');
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
    cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');
  });

  it('locks the account after 5 failed attempts', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
      cy.get('[data-cy=email]').clear().type('manager@bizlevate.test');
      cy.get('[data-cy=password]').clear().type('wrongpassword');
      cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
    }
    cy.get('[data-cy=login-error]').should('contain', 'Account locked');
  });
});
```

This handful of tests is precisely what should exercise the real form — nothing here can be meaningfully shortcut, because the login mechanism itself is the subject under test.

## When programmatic login is the right choice — everything else

Every other test in the suite that merely requires a logged-in session as a precondition, but isn't testing the login mechanism itself, should use programmatic login. This is the overwhelming majority of a real application's test suite:

```js
describe('Leave approvals', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
```
`cy.loginAs('manager')`;    // programmatic, cached via `cy.session()` — Part 7.2

```js
    cy.visit('/leave/approvals');
  });

  it('shows pending requests', () => { /* ... this test is about leave approvals, not login */ });
});
```

## The token-based programmatic pattern, complete

Bringing together everything from 6.1, 7.1, and 7.2 into one canonical, reusable implementation:

```js
// cypress/support/commands.js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(
    role,
    () => {
      cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
        const { email, password } = users[role];
        cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', { email, password }).then((response) => {
          window.localStorage.setItem('authToken', response.body.token);
          window.localStorage.setItem('refreshToken', response.body.refreshToken);
        });
      });
    },
    {
      validate() {
        const token = window.localStorage.getItem('authToken');
        cy.request({
          url: '/api/auth/me',
          headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
          failOnStatusCode: false,
        }).its('status').should('eq', 200);
      },
      cacheAcrossSpecs: true,
    }
  );
});

// cypress/support/index.d.ts
declare global {
  namespace Cypress {
    interface Chainable {
      loginAs(role: 'admin' | 'manager' | 'employee'): Chainable<void>;
    }
  }
}
```

This single command — cached, validated, typed — is what every test in the suite that merely needs to be logged in should call. It's the payoff for everything built up across this Part.

## The cookie-based variant

For apps using server-set session cookies rather than a client-stored JWT, the pattern simplifies, since `cy.request()` running in the same browser context applies Set-Cookie automatically (as noted in 6.1):

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(role, () => {
    cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
      cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', users[role]);
      // no manual storage step needed — the session cookie is applied automatically
    });
  }, {
    validate() {
      cy.request({ url: '/api/auth/me', failOnStatusCode: false }).its('status').should('eq', 200);
    },
  });
});
```

## SSO / OAuth — the case that resists a clean shortcut

Recall the SSO scenario from Part 0.6: your app redirects to login.okta.com (or Azure AD, Google Workspace) and back. A pure `cy.request()`-based login often isn't available here at all — many SSO providers don't expose a simple username/password API endpoint by design, precisely because they want authentication to happen through their own controlled UI.
Three realistic strategies, in order of preference:
1. Ask the SSO provider for a test/service-account bypass. Many enterprise identity providers offer a non-interactive grant type (client credentials, or a dedicated test-tenant API) specifically for automated testing. If this exists, it's functionally equivalent to the token-based pattern above — get a token programmatically, seed it, skip the UI entirely.
2. Cache a real UI-driven SSO login via `cy.session()`. When no programmatic bypass exists, fall back to what 7.2 already demonstrated — a full UI login inside the session setup function, using `cy.origin()` (Part 9.1) to cross into the SSO domain:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(role, () => {
    cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
      const { email, password } = users[role];
      cy.visit('/login');   // app redirects to the SSO provider

      cy.origin('https://login.okta.com', { args: { email, password } }, ({ email, password }) => {
        cy.get('#username').type(email);
        cy.get('#password').type(password);
        cy.get('button[type=submit]').click();
      });

      cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');   // back on the app's own origin
    });
  });
});
```

This still only pays the real (slow, cross-origin) cost once per role per run — `cy.session()`'s caching benefit applies just as fully here as it does to the pure-API case, because the caching layer doesn't care how expensive or complex the setup function was, only that it ran once and its result can be restored.
3. Stub the SSO provider's response entirely via cy.intercept(), if your architecture allows it — treating the identity provider as an external dependency to mock rather than a real system to authenticate against, similar in spirit to the payment-widget stubbing discussed in Part 3.6. This trades realism for speed and reliability, and is a defensible choice specifically for the large majority of tests that need a logged-in session and don't care that the SSO handshake itself was faked.

## Multi-role testing within one spec

A pattern that ties 7.1 through 7.4 together concretely — verifying role-based access control requires genuinely switching identities mid-suite, which `cy.session()`'s per-role keying (7.2) makes cheap:

```js
describe('Role-based access to payroll', () => {
  it('employees cannot access payroll admin', () => {
    cy.loginAs('employee');
    cy.visit('/payroll/admin', { failOnStatusCode: false });
    cy.get('[data-cy=forbidden]').should('be.visible');
  });

  it('managers can view but not run payroll', () => {
    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit('/payroll/admin');
    cy.get('[data-cy=run-payroll-btn]').should('not.exist');
    cy.get('[data-cy=payroll-summary]').should('be.visible');
  });

  it('admins can run payroll', () => {
    cy.loginAs('admin');
    cy.visit('/payroll/admin');
    cy.get('[data-cy=run-payroll-btn]').should('be.visible').and('be.enabled');
  });
});
```

Each `cy.loginAs()` call here restores a distinct cached session almost instantly — none of the cost of three separate real logins, while still genuinely exercising three separate authenticated identities.

## Testing logout

Logout deserves its own honest test, and it's naturally a UI-driven one since the logout mechanism itself is what's under test:

```js
it('clears the session on logout and redirects to login', () => {
  cy.loginAs('employee');
  cy.visit('/dashboard');

  cy.get('[data-cy=logout]').click();

  cy.url().should('include', '/login');
  cy.window().its('localStorage.authToken').should('be.undefined');
  cy.getCookie('session_id').should('be.null');

  // and confirm a subsequent protected-route visit is actually blocked, not just visually redirected
  cy.visit('/dashboard');
  cy.url().should('include', '/login');
});
```

That last assertion matters — it distinguishes "the UI looks logged out" from "the app actually enforces the logged-out state," which is a meaningfully stronger and more honest test.

## Comparison

The strategic split — UI login for testing login itself, programmatic login (cached) for everything else — is identical in Playwright, built on `storageState` instead of `cy.session()`, with the same SSO fallback options (a test-tenant bypass, a cached real UI flow via page.context().`storageState`(), or stubbing the identity provider via page.route()). Playwright's cross-origin handling for the SSO case is meaningfully simpler, needing no `cy.origin()`-equivalent wrapper at all, since Playwright was never origin-bound to begin with (Part 0.6) — the SSO login script in a Playwright global-setup file reads as one continuous flow with no special block required for the identity-provider domain.
Selenium implements the same UI-vs-programmatic split via whatever HTTP client is paired alongside it for the programmatic path (as in Part 6.1's comparison), and handles cookie-seeding manually via driver.add_cookie() after an initial driver.get() to the target domain — functionally similar to Cypress's `onBeforeLoad` pattern in spirit, but with no equivalent to `cy.session()`'s automatic caching-with-validation, meaning teams either hand-roll their own session-reuse layer or, commonly, simply accept the cost of a real login per test.
